    "q-95111": {
      "dom_id": "step-q95111",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Given that $q(x,y)=xy,$ find $\\frac{∂q}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates.",
      "worked_solution": "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $q(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂q}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂q}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂q}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂q}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $q$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂q}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂x} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂q}{∂y} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂\\theta } & =-(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
